00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.880
Now back to the video! First, let’s&nbsp;
recap what diffusion models are.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:49.120
For more details for example on classifier-free&nbsp;
guidance, do check out our previous videos on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.240
this. But, as far as our explanations&nbsp;
of diffusion models in general go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.880
this video has the technically&nbsp;
most correct version, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.880
we cleared up a confusion that Ms.&nbsp;
Coffee Bean had this whole time.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.480
But let’s not get ahead of ourselves and&nbsp;
talk about diffusion models first! The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.240
diffusion process is where you diffuse&nbsp;
more and more noise into your image.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:20.160
So, you take an image, and in t steps,&nbsp;
you gradually add more noise to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.360
until at the last timestep t, the&nbsp;
image is approximately just noise.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.520
Diffusion models go in the opposite direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.640
in other words, they learn to&nbsp;
reverse the diffusion process.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.200
When we are trying to generate&nbsp;
an image with a diffusion model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.720
we follow each of these t steps and&nbsp;
reduce the noise gradually, step by step.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:50.800
For this, we use the same neural network,&nbsp;
so just one neural network, usually a U-Net,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:56.800
to go from step t to step t-1 and here,&nbsp;
for visualization purposes we have chosen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:04.480
step t to be a noisy dog and step t-1&nbsp;
to be a less noisy version of the dog.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.720
Short sidenote on U-Nets: A UNet is a&nbsp;
convolution-based neural network that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:17.280
downsampling an image into a lower dimensional&nbsp;
representation and reconstructs it during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:24.400
upsampling. The downsampling and upsampling stacks&nbsp;
of layers communicate through skip connections.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.280
And now, what exactly is the&nbsp;
input to the neural network?&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.600
It’s an image at step t and the neural&nbsp;
network’s output is the total noise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.320
that should be subtracted from the&nbsp;
noisy version of the image at step t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:48.000
to reconstruct the original image,&nbsp;
so the clear picture of the dog.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.560
Wait, let’s untangle this a bit and&nbsp;
recapitulate the whole diffusion thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.680
from the beginning because it is an important&nbsp;
detail Ms. Coffee Bean got wrong so far

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:00.880
(of course, I knew the&nbsp;
correct version all the time).

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:07.600
Unfortunately, the papers are not so clear about&nbsp;
it. How it is done becomes clear from the code.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:14.000
The diffusion process is all about going from&nbsp;
a little noise to more noise. The backward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:19.600
diffusion process is the reverse, and this is&nbsp;
what we do with the help of diffusion models.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:25.440
And the diffusion model itself, so this&nbsp;
one U-Net that we apply at each step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.280
gets a noisy image at step t and predicts&nbsp;
the whole noise that the image contains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.360
and not just the noise we need&nbsp;
to subtract to get from t to t-1.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.600
It’s just that we do not trust the model enough&nbsp;
to just subtract this whole noise in one go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:47.360
so at each step, we extract just a fraction of&nbsp;
the total noise from the image at timestep t.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:52.640
Reasons for going step by step is&nbsp;
that we break down a hard problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.440
of generating an image from just noise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:01.680
into t steps, where each step can correct and&nbsp;
improve upon the previous one, especially when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:07.360
the whole time we are trying to inject textual&nbsp;
information when doing text-to-image generation.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.280
It's easier to inject textual&nbsp;
information gradually than all at once.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.680
Ok, so this was basically an&nbsp;
erratum to our previous videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.560
where we thought that at each step, the&nbsp;
model predicts only a fraction of the noise.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:25.440
But actually, the diffusion model predicts&nbsp;
the total amount of noise from which we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:31.760
then subtract only a fraction to give it&nbsp;
afterwards yet another go at denoising.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:38.720
But now, how does the text come into play? It&nbsp;
is injected into the whole process in two ways:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.480
First as input to the diffusion&nbsp;
model by concatenating the text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:46.880
representation coming from a language&nbsp;
transformer to the image input.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:53.520
And second, through cross-attention, letting the&nbsp;
U-Net attention layers attend to the text tokens.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.280
Now, what about Stable Diffusion,&nbsp;
and how does this work?&nbsp;

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.800
The idea behind stable diffusion was&nbsp;
introduced in this CVPR paper called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:07.520
“High-Resolution Image Synthesis&nbsp;
with Latent Diffusion Models”&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.840
from Heidelberg folk [proud]&nbsp;
who now moved to Munich [sad].

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:14.240
The authors proposed latent diffusion models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.720
or short LDMs, to address a&nbsp;
shortcoming of diffusion models:

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:25.760
When trying to generate a large image,
such as a 1024 x 1024 image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:31.600
then the U-Net in here, would have to take&nbsp;
in a 1024 x 1024-dimensional noise grid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:33.600
and produce an image out of it.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.680
As you can imagine, this can become&nbsp;
really expensive for one diffusion step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.520
and one has to do it t times,&nbsp;
where t can be something like 150.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:46.160
What people did so far to circumvent&nbsp;
this high dimensionality problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:49.200
as we have seen in the GLIDE paper for example,&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:56.000
is that they actually train their diffusion&nbsp;
model on much smaller images, like 256 x 256&nbsp;

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:01.120
and then have an extra neural network&nbsp;
that learned to upsample and sharpen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:08.320
256 x 256 to higher resolution, for&nbsp;
example. And this is one way to do it.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:13.760
But LDMs take another approach.
The core concept of LDMs is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.560
surrender the idea of working in the image&nbsp;
space and work on a latent space instead.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:24.400
You may think of it as of an autoencoder&nbsp;
encompassing the diffusion model, as follows:

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:29.040
For diffusion models, we had the original&nbsp;
image to which we added more and more noise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:34.480
But now, we do not work with the image itself,&nbsp;
but with a lower-dimensional representation of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:39.520
because we take the image and run it through&nbsp;
an encoder, which is basically a VQGAN.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.080
This encoder also has a decoder&nbsp;
to reconstruct the image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.120
This encoder and decoder&nbsp;
are trained together first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:52.160
to encode the image into a lower dimensional&nbsp;
space and then reconstruct the image from it.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:57.120
Now, instead of applying noise onto&nbsp;
the image for the diffusion step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.840
we apply noise onto the lower-dimensional&nbsp;
representation of the data.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:05.920
And for the backward diffusion, the&nbsp;
U-Net works with this representation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:10.640
instead of the whole image. Since the&nbsp;
image representation is lower dimensional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:14.640
the U-Net does not have a&nbsp;
lot of heavy lifting to do.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:20.080
Even more, by compressing the image first&nbsp;
through the encoder, the encoder decoder can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:27.840
take care of image details and let the diffusion&nbsp;
model focus on the important image semantics.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.840
This makes LDMs much faster than usual&nbsp;
diffusion models, so this enables us to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:40.800
Stable Diffusion on laptop hardware, instead&nbsp;
of clusters or you know, OpenAI servers! 😉

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:46.080
We hope you see how clever, but similar&nbsp;
LDMs are to what previous work like GLIDE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:51.520
did: GLIDE, took an image, downsampled&nbsp;
it from let’s say, 1024 x 1024 pixels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:58.880
to 256 x 256 resolution with a usual image&nbsp;
downsampling algorithm, staying in image space.

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:03.840
Then they ran diffusion and upsampled the&nbsp;
result to get a high-resolution image.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:11.200
Now with LDMs, instead of a standard downsampling&nbsp;
algorithm that produces low-resolution images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:17.520
the LDM encoder takes a high-resolution image&nbsp;
and embeds it into a compressed code of its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:21.360
that captures the semantics of the&nbsp;
image, but in a space that does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.800
not look to us like the original image anymore.&nbsp;
And the diffusion model can take it from there.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:32.320
As you can see from the paper, the author’s&nbsp;
initial work on LDMs was producing images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:38.240
of human faces and objects. This is because&nbsp;
the model whose output we see in Figure 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:40.880
was trained on human faces and objects.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:45.520
And you know, what kind of data comes into the&nbsp;
diffusion model, this is what they also produce.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:52.720
What makes Stable Diffusion so special and fit&nbsp;
for art generation, is that it was actually an LDM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.720 --> 00:11:00.320
trained on a core dataset consisting of, we cite&nbsp;
“LAION-Aesthetics, a soon to be released subset of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:08.080
LAION 5B. LAION-Aesthetics was created with a new&nbsp;
CLIP-based model that filtered LAION-5B based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:14.160
how “beautiful” an image was, building on ratings&nbsp;
from the alpha testers of Stable Diffusion”.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:19.040
And this different training data makes&nbsp;
it have its own style that is different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:23.680
from other text-to-image generators&nbsp;
like DALL-E 2, Midjourney or Imagen.

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:26.400
So to sum up: what is now Stable Diffusion?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:30.400
It is basically the work that follows&nbsp;
the Latent Diffusion Models paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:37.600
where authors of the paper teamed with StabilityAI&nbsp;
and communities such as Eleuther AI and LAION.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:41.760
Now, with Stable Diffusion we have an&nbsp;
image generator tuned to produce art,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:47.680
as it was trained on “beautiful” images,&nbsp;
which were often produced by human artists.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:54.240
It will be interesting to see what kind of new&nbsp;
copyright law these AI generations will spawn.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:12:00.880
Some artists feel bad to see that AI can now&nbsp;
do something in seconds that took them hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:07.120
to make. But other artists consider the AI to be&nbsp;
a sort of tool, which still needs human input.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:13.520
And the human hours that go into diffusion-based&nbsp;
image generation is not a neglectable amount.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:18.400
Just think about the hours one can spend&nbsp;
in tuning the text prompt and waiting for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:24.240
the right result to come out, only to notice&nbsp;
that the astronaut is riding the horse again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.840
when I actually wanted the horse&nbsp;
riding the astronaut on the moon!

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:33.760
If you are interested in using Stable Diffusion&nbsp;
yourself, then check out the diffusers library&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:36.480
or any other helpful tutorials out there.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:41.440
It’s really amazing what the community&nbsp;
has put out in such a short amount of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:45.520
in terms of documentation and
in terms of generations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:50.720
Just look at this Twitter user bringing&nbsp;
their 4 year-old’s sketches to life.

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.280
Wow. Do you think this is creativity?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:59.120
We’re so curious to know what you think about this
so, we’ll be watching the comment section closely.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:04.880
Ms. Coffee Bean, it is time to say&nbsp;
goodbye. See you in the next video!

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:15.600
Okay, bye!

